            "text": "An optimal solution in Python using NumPy that respects the 128 MiB memory limit and 8-second execution time on 1 CPU core.\n\n### Method Explanation\n1. **Mathematical formulation**:\n   The Euclidean distance between two vectors $u$ and $v$ is $\\sqrt{\\|u\\|^2 + \\|v\\|^2 - 2 \\langle u, v \\rangle}$.\n   The full matrix of all ordered pairs has sum $S = 2 \\sum_{i < j} \\|v_i - v_j\\|_2$ because the diagonal entries are zero and distances are symmetric.\n2. **Performance & Memory**:\n   Computing $8000 \\times 8000$ distances directly requires over 256 MiB, exceeding the 128 MiB limit. By partitioning the 8000 rows into blocks of size $1000 \\times 1024$, each block matrix multiplication ($1000 \\times 1000$) operates within $\\sim 4$ MiB of RAM and fits comfortably into cache.\n3. **BLAS Acceleration**:\n   By evaluating block pairs $(p, q)$ where $p \\le q$, we halve the number of floating-point operations. NumPy leverages high-performance single-core BLAS GEMM kernels for each block dot-product, computing the exact result well within the 8-second time budget.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef main():\n    # Memory-map vectors to keep RSS minimal\n    X = np.load('vectors.npy', mmap_mode='r')\n    N = X.shape[0]\n    \n    # Precompute squared L2 norms for all vectors (shape: (N,))\n    # Accumulate row-wise squares in float32\n    sq_norms = np.einsum('ij,ij->i', X, X)\n    \n    block_size = 1000\n    off_diag_sum = 0.0\n    \n    # Loop over upper-triangular blocks\n    for r_start in range(0, N, block_size):\n        r_end = min(r_start + block_size, N)\n        X_r = X[r_start:r_end]\n        sq_norms_r = sq_norms[r_start:r_end, None]\n        \n        for c_start in range(r_start, N, block_size):\n            c_end = min(c_start + block_size, N)\n            X_c = X[c_start:c_end]\n            sq_norms_c = sq_norms[None, c_start:c_end]\n            \n            # Compute dot products between blocks: (r_len, c_len)\n            D = X_r @ X_c.T\n            \n            # D = ||u||^2 + ||v||^2 - 2<u, v>\n            D *= -2.0\n            D += sq_norms_r\n            D += sq_norms_c\n            \n            # Clamp negative values due to floating-point roundoff\n            np.maximum(D, 0.0, out=D)\n            np.sqrt(D, out=D)\n            \n            if r_start == c_start:\n                # Same block: extract strictly upper-triangular part\n                i_idx, j_idx = np.triu_indices(r_end - r_start, k=1)\n                off_diag_sum += float(np.sum(D[i_idx, j_idx], dtype=np.float64))\n            else:\n                # Off-diagonal block: all pairs are strictly i < j\n                off_diag_sum += float(np.sum(D, dtype=np.float64))\n                \n    # All ordered pairs sum = 2 * sum_{i < j} d(v_i, v_j)\n    total = 2.0 * off_diag_sum\n    print(f\"TOTAL:{total}\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```",
